### Summary of the Rendering Stack

To keep this organized as you build, your client-side draw loop should strictly follow this painter's algorithm from bottom to top:

| Render Order | Layer Purpose | Features Handled |
| --- | --- | --- |
| **1. Baseline** | The pre-rendered map image (Ground Floor). | Environment graphics. |
| **2. Dynamic Base** | Ground items, walkable furniture, player/NPC sprites. | Positional data, Elevation ($Z=0$). |
| **3. Structural** | Walls, multi-floor overlaps, stairs. | Second floor visibility ($Z=1$). |
| **4. Masking** | Fog of War, un-discovered room overlays. | First-time discovery, Window haze. |
| **5. Environment** | The composite mask for weather, day/night cycles. | Rain, Snow, Night/Day, Roofs. |
| **6. Lighting** | The additive light map for torches/fireplaces. | Dynamic interactive lighting. |

Are you planning to build this custom renderer entirely using the standard HTML5 2D Canvas API, or are you leaning toward a WebGL framework (like PixiJS) to handle these compositing masks and shaders natively on the GPU?

You *can* use six different physical `<canvas>` elements stacked on top of each other using CSS absolute positioning, but that is generally considered an anti-pattern for a game engine. It splits your state across the DOM, makes resizing a nightmare, and hurts performance because the browser has to composite multiple independent canvas elements together on every frame.

Instead, the industry-standard way to do this is to use **one single canvas** but utilize **off-screen canvas buffers** (or WebGL layers) to compute your masks before drawing the final output to the screen.

Here is exactly how you handle those six rendering steps efficiently inside a single render loop.

---

## The Monolithic Canvas with Off-Screen Buffers

Think of your main screen canvas as a clean piece of paper. Instead of having six sheets of paper stacked up, you have **one** sheet of paper, and you use temporary "stencils" (off-screen canvases) to paint your lighting, fog, and weather effects directly onto it in sequence.

You only need **three** canvas objects in your entire code architecture:

1. `mainCanvas`: The actual element visible in the HTML DOM.
2. `lightBuffer`: An invisible, off-screen canvas used exclusively to draw torch circles and ambient shadows.
3. `maskBuffer`: An invisible, off-screen canvas used to draw Fog of War and roof visibility shapes.

---

## Step-by-Step Render Loop Implementation

Here is how your core TypeScript render function looks, executed inside a `requestAnimationFrame` loop. It runs all 6 layers sequentially onto the single screen context.

```typescript
class MapRenderer {
  private mainCtx: CanvasRenderingContext2D;
  private lightCtx: CanvasRenderingContext2D;
  private maskCtx: CanvasRenderingContext2D;

  // Global game assets
  private mapImage: HTMLImageElement;

  public render(gameState: GameState) {
    const ctx = this.mainCtx;
    const width = ctx.canvas.width;
    const height = ctx.canvas.height;

    // Clear everything from the previous frame
    ctx.clearRect(0, 0, width, height);
    this.lightCtx.clearRect(0, 0, width, height);
    this.maskCtx.clearRect(0, 0, width, height);

    // ==========================================
    // LAYER 1 & 3: Background & Architecture
    // ==========================================
    // Draw your scanned grid map artwork baseline
    ctx.drawImage(this.mapImage, 0, 0);

    // ==========================================
    // LAYER 2: Dynamic Entities (Y-Sorted)
    // ==========================================
    // Sort characters/items by Y coordinate so they depth-occlude correctly
    const sortedEntities = [...gameState.npcs, ...gameState.players].sort((a, b) => a.y - b.y);
    for (const entity of sortedEntities) {
      ctx.drawImage(entity.sprite, entity.pixelX, entity.pixelY);
    }

    // ==========================================
    // LAYER 4 & 5: Masking (Fog of War, Discovery, Roofs)
    // ==========================================
    // 1. Fill the invisible mask buffer with solid black (undiscovered)
    this.maskCtx.fillStyle = "rgba(0, 0, 0, 1.0)";
    this.maskCtx.fillRect(0, 0, width, height);

    // 2. Punch holes of visibility in the mask where the player has discovered rooms
    this.maskCtx.globalCompositeOperation = 'destination-out';
    for (const roomId of gameState.discoveredRooms) {
      const roomRects = this.getRoomPixelRectangles(roomId);
      for (const rect of roomRects) {
        this.maskCtx.fillRect(rect.x, rect.y, rect.width, rect.height);
      }
    }
    // Reset composite operation for the mask buffer
    this.maskCtx.globalCompositeOperation = 'source-over';

    // 3. Draw the off-screen mask buffer directly onto our visible main canvas
    ctx.drawImage(this.maskCtx.canvas, 0, 0);

    // ==========================================
    // LAYER 5 (Part 2): Global Weather Overlay
    // ==========================================
    if (gameState.weather === 'RAIN') {
      this.renderRainParticles(ctx);
    }

    // ==========================================
    // LAYER 6: Dynamic Lighting & Day/Night
    // ==========================================
    // 1. Fill light buffer with the ambient darkness level (e.g., Night or Dark Interior)
    this.lightCtx.fillStyle = gameState.ambientLightColor; // e.g., "rgba(10, 15, 30, 0.85)"
    this.lightCtx.fillRect(0, 0, width, height);

    // 2. Change composition to change overlapping pixels to transparent white light
    this.lightCtx.globalCompositeOperation = 'destination-out';

    for (const torch of gameState.activeTorches) {
      const gradient = this.lightCtx.createRadialGradient(
        torch.pixelX, torch.pixelY, 0,
        torch.pixelX, torch.pixelY, torch.radiusPixels
      );
      gradient.addColorStop(0, "rgba(0, 0, 0, 1.0)");   // Completely clear center
      gradient.addColorStop(1, "rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.0)");   // Fades out to dark ambient

      this.lightCtx.fillStyle = gradient;
      this.lightCtx.beginPath();
      this.lightCtx.arc(torch.pixelX, torch.pixelY, torch.radiusPixels, 0, Math.PI * 2);
      this.lightCtx.fill();
    }
    this.lightCtx.globalCompositeOperation = 'source-over';

    // 3. Composite the darkness-and-lights over the entire screen using Multiply
    ctx.globalCompositeOperation = 'multiply';
    ctx.drawImage(this.lightCtx.canvas, 0, 0);

    // CRITICAL: Reset the main canvas blend mode back to normal for the next frame loop!
    ctx.globalCompositeOperation = 'source-over';
  }
}

```

